---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { copy } from '../../../data';
const c = copy.en;
---
<BaseLayout title="DogSpace — technical case study" description="DogSpace: a social network for people and dogs, built around a shared Actor model, delegated authority, and server-side authorization." lang="en" alternate="/projects/dogspace/">
  <article class="container case">
    <a class="back" href="/en/#projects">← {c.home}</a>
    <header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING CASE · PERSONAL PROJECT</p><h1>DogSpace</h1><p class="lead">A social network where people and dogs have their own profiles—and every social action has a clear author.</p><p class="status">In development · product flows and interfaces are being built incrementally</p><p class="stack">PHP 8.5 · Laravel 13 · PostgreSQL 17 · Inertia 3 · Vue 3 · TypeScript · Tailwind CSS 4 · Docker</p></header>

    <section><h2>The problem</h2><p>A conventional social network is centered on a human account. DogSpace needs another option: a follow, post, or reaction may come from the person or from a dog they are authorized to represent.</p><p>That is why people and dogs have separate social profiles. The model expresses relationships between people and pets without conflating the user with the subject of their actions. It also provides a foundation for privacy, delegation, and auditability.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Architecture</h2><p>The project is a Laravel modular monolith: functional areas remain parts of one application, database, and deployment lifecycle. Domain Actions coordinate state-changing operations; Policies and Gate handle permission checks for specific actions.</p><div class="areas"><article><h3>Identity</h3><p>Users, dogs, social profiles, and authority relationships.</p></article><article><h3>Social Graph</h3><p>Follows, access requests, and blocks between social profiles.</p></article><article><h3>Content &amp; Feed</h3><p>Posts, comments, reactions, media, and feeds.</p></article><article><h3>Notifications</h3><p>In-app notifications with access rechecked when delivered.</p></article><article><h3>Moderation</h3><p>An MVP for reports, content decisions, and audit history.</p></article><article><h3>Administration</h3><p>Basic platform roles and announcements.</p></article></div><p class="small muted">These areas are parts of one Laravel application, not separate services.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Key engineering decisions</h2><div class="decisions"><article><h3>Actor is the social author</h3><p>Graph and content actions belong to an Actor: a person’s or dog’s profile. The User who actually made the request is recorded separately.</p></article><article><h3>Context is not permission</h3><p>The selected active Actor is stored in the server session for interface continuity. The server revalidates availability and permission to perform each action.</p></article><article><h3>Ownership differs from management</h3><p>Owner and co_owner describe ownership; manager and caregiver describe delegated management. Caregiver social actions and profile editing are granted independently.</p></article><article><h3>Privacy is checked server-side</h3><p>Access to profiles, posts, and photos depends on current visibility, follows, blocks, and authority—not only on what the client displays.</p></article><article><h3>Media stays private</h3><p>Post photos are stored on a private disk. The server rechecks access to the post and verifies that the file belongs to it before serving media.</p></article></div></section>

    <section class="actor-deep"><p class="eyebrow">DEEPER DIVE</p><h2>Architectural decision: Actor</h2><p>If follows and posts referenced User directly, a dog’s activity would have to be represented as the owner’s activity with an extra label. That obscures authorship: following a dog specifically or distinguishing its voice from a person’s would be awkward. Actor gives both subject types a shared social interface while Dog and User retain their own domain data.</p>
      <figure class="diagram"><figcaption>Action subject and server-validated authority</figcaption><div class="diagram-scroll"><svg viewBox="0 0 900 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="diag-title diag-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="diag-title">A user acts through a selected social Actor</title><desc id="diag-desc">A User selects an available Actor in Actor Context. The User Actor represents their own profile. The Dog Actor represents a Dog profile; the ability to act as the dog depends on active owner, co_owner, manager, or caregiver relationships and permissions. The selected Actor does not replace server-side authorization.</desc><defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="7" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L8 4L0 8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></marker></defs><g class="node"><rect x="24" y="35" width="170" height="70" rx="10"/><text x="109" y="66" text-anchor="middle" class="title">User</text><text x="109" y="87" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">authenticated person</text></g><g class="node"><rect x="270" y="35" width="225" height="70" rx="10"/><text x="382" y="66" text-anchor="middle" class="title">Actor Context</text><text x="382" y="87" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">session selection · revalidated</text></g><path class="edge" d="M194 70H263" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/><text x="228" y="59" text-anchor="middle" class="label">select</text><g class="node"><rect x="590" y="35" width="260" height="70" rx="10"/><text x="720" y="66" text-anchor="middle" class="title">Actor: person's profile</text><text x="720" y="87" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">social identity for User</text></g><path class="edge" d="M495 60H583" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/><text x="539" y="49" text-anchor="middle" class="label">self</text><g class="node"><rect x="270" y="205" width="225" height="70" rx="10"/><text x="382" y="236" text-anchor="middle" class="title">Actor: dog's profile</text><text x="382" y="257" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">social identity for Dog</text></g><g class="node"><rect x="590" y="205" width="260" height="70" rx="10"/><text x="720" y="236" text-anchor="middle" class="title">Dog</text><text x="720" y="257" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">profile and domain data</text></g><path class="edge" d="M382 105V197" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/><text x="394" y="157" class="label">available if authorized</text><path class="edge" d="M495 240H583" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/><text x="539" y="229" text-anchor="middle" class="label">represents</text><path class="relation" d="M194 95C225 160 226 220 263 235"/><path class="relation" d="M194 102C218 230 222 276 263 256"/><text x="590" y="318" class="sub">Policies + transactional Actions + PostgreSQL constraints</text></svg></div><ul class="diagram-legend"><li><span class="line" aria-hidden="true"></span>User → Actor Context: select the active context</li><li><span class="line dashed" aria-hidden="true"></span>User → Dog Actor: active ownership or management role</li><li>The Dog Actor represents the dog's social profile; permission is checked separately for each action.</li></ul></figure>
      <h3>How delegation works</h3><p>A User may act through their own Actor or select a Dog Actor when they have an active authority relationship. Owner and co_owner live in ownership relationships; manager and caregiver live in management relationships. They have different bases of access and different lifecycles.</p><ul><li><strong>owner:</strong> the primary owner; manages membership and the profile within Policy rules.</li><li><strong>co_owner:</strong> a co-owner; ownership does not automatically grant authority over other members.</li><li><strong>manager:</strong> a delegated role with the management capabilities defined by the system.</li><li><strong>caregiver:</strong> a delegated role; `edit_profile` and `perform_social_actions` are granted independently, and a missing permission means denial.</li></ul><p>The active Actor identifier in the session is context selection, not a credential. On every request the server recomputes available Actors, and Policies authorize the specific operation. A revoked relationship therefore stops granting access even if an old identifier remains in the session.</p><p>Relationship changes go through transactional Actions; concurrent ownership changes lock the Dog record and recheck conditions. PostgreSQL CHECK constraints, foreign keys, and partial unique indexes prevent some invalid states from being stored—for example duplicate active roles or a second primary owner. The database complements application checks; it does not replace business operations that must create related records atomically.</p><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> one Actor abstraction simplifies the Social Graph and authorship, but adds relationship tables, context resolution, and repeated checks. Authority cannot be represented by a single flag: ownership, management, and caregiver capabilities must be modeled and verified separately. That complexity keeps the social author distinct from the human who performed the request.</p>
    </section>

    <section><h2>Security and testing</h2><p>Policies and Gate centralize access checks. For private pages, follows, and media, the server considers current relationships and blocks; file paths are not exposed to the client. User/dog creation and their Actors, invitations, and relationship changes use transactions. PostgreSQL constraints protect uniqueness and valid roles at the storage layer.</p><p>The repository includes feature checks for Actor Context, User and Dog profile privacy, owner and manager invitations, Social Graph, posts, and media. Both allowed flows and denied access are covered. This describes project tests, not production operating metrics.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Current state and next steps</h2><p>Identity, Social Graph, Content, feeds, in-app notifications, MVP moderation, and basic administration are implemented in the repository. The Inertia interface supports invitation-based registration, creating and listing dog profiles, viewing and editing a profile, uploading an avatar, selecting the active Actor, and inviting co-owners and managers. Open self-registration is disabled; accounts are created by invitation.</p><p>The project roadmap lists transfer of primary ownership, account deletion handling, expanded moderation and notifications, Help Dogs, and a PWA as future work. DogSpace remains in development; no public production launch or metrics are claimed.</p><p class="note">The repository is private. Source code and internal data are not published; I’m happy to discuss architectural choices and selected implementation details in a technical interview.</p></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .case{max-width:820px;padding-block:44px 96px}.back{color:var(--accent);text-decoration:none}.hero{padding:52px 0 40px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--muted)}h1{font-size:clamp(2.6rem,7vw,4.5rem);line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.04em;margin:18px 0}.lead{font-size:clamp(1.2rem,2.3vw,1.45rem);line-height:1.5;color:var(--text);max-width:740px}.status,.stack,.small,.muted{color:var(--muted)}.status{margin-top:24px}.stack{font-size:14px;border-top:1px solid var(--border);padding-top:18px;margin-top:22px}.case section{padding-top:52px}.case h2{font-size:clamp(1.65rem,3vw,2.15rem);line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:-.025em;margin:0 0 20px}.case section p,.case li{line-height:1.75}.case section p{color:var(--muted)}.case h3{font-size:18px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 8px}.areas,.decisions{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:12px;margin-top:24px}.areas article,.decisions article{border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;background:var(--surface);padding:18px}.areas p,.decisions p{margin:0;font-size:15px}.small{font-size:13px}.actor-deep{padding:26px!important;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;background:var(--surface);margin-top:56px}.actor-deep h2{margin-top:4px!important}.actor-deep h3{margin-top:30px}.actor-deep ul{padding-left:22px}.actor-deep li{margin:10px 0;color:var(--muted)}.diagram{border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:12px;margin:30px 0 18px;padding:20px;background:var(--bg)}.diagram figcaption{font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px}.diagram-scroll{overflow-x:auto}.diagram svg{display:block;width:100%;min-width:680px;height:auto;color:var(--accent)}.diagram .node rect{fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--border);stroke-width:1.5}.diagram .title{fill:var(--text);font:600 16px system-ui,sans-serif}.diagram .sub{fill:var(--muted);font:12px system-ui,sans-serif}.diagram .label{fill:var(--muted);font:11px system-ui,sans-serif}.diagram .edge{fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.6}.diagram .relation{fill:none;stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.25;stroke-dasharray:5 4}.diagram-legend{list-style:none;padding:12px 0 0;margin:14px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)}.diagram-legend li{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;margin:8px 0}.diagram-legend .line{display:inline-block;width:24px;height:0;border-top:2px solid var(--accent);flex:none}.diagram-legend .dashed{border-top:2px dashed var(--muted)}.note{border-top:1px solid var(--border);padding-top:20px;margin-top:30px}.case strong{color:var(--text)}@media(max-width:620px){.case{padding-block:28px 64px}.hero{padding-top:38px}.case section{padding-top:40px}.areas,.decisions{grid-template-columns:1fr}.actor-deep{padding:18px!important;margin-top:42px}.diagram{padding:14px}.diagram svg{min-width:680px}}
</style>
